---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy · b-mobile for Blipfoto · b-oss"
  description="Privacy policy for b-mobile for Blipfoto — the Android app for browsing and posting to your Blipfoto journal."
  narrow
>
  <Hero label="Privacy hero" title="Privacy Policy — b-mobile for Blipfoto">b-mobile for Blipfoto is an Android app for browsing, posting to and getting notifications
      from your Blipfoto journal. This policy explains what data the app handles, where it goes,
      and how to have it removed.</Hero>

  <section
    class="block"
    style="border-top: none; padding-top: 0"
    data-screen-label="Privacy body"
  >
    <p><em>Last updated: 11 October 2026</em></p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      b-mobile for Blipfoto is an open-source project, part of b-oss and led by Ian Stevenson.
      It is published on the Google Play Store on behalf of the development team by Salient
      Point Consulting Ltd. It is an independent app built on Blipfoto's public API, and is not
      affiliated with or endorsed by Blipfoto. Questions about this policy can be sent to
      <a href="mailto:info@b-oss.dev">info@b-oss.dev</a> or through the
      <a href="contact.html">contact page</a>, whose form is delivered by
      <a href="https://web3forms.com/privacy">Web3Forms</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your Blipfoto access tokens, held in Android's secure storage (the Keystore). They are
        used to talk to Blipfoto and are never shown or logged.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your app settings, the list of members you have hidden, drafts, and photos queued for
        upload.
      </li>
      <li>
        A cache of pictures you have looked at, which is refreshed after 15 minutes and which
        Android can clear at any time.
      </li>
      <li>
        A record of the last request that failed, for troubleshooting. It stays on the device.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of this is sent to the developer. Uninstalling the app removes it. On Android 10 and
      later, photos you download from your own entries are saved to your phone's Pictures folder
      (<code>Pictures/b-mobile</code>) and stay there after uninstalling, like any other saved
      picture.
    </p>

    <h2>What is sent to Blipfoto</h2>
    <p>
      The app is a client for Blipfoto, so everything you do in it goes to Blipfoto's servers
      under Blipfoto's own privacy policy: signing in, browsing, posting and editing entries,
      uploading photos, comments, stars, favourites, follows and reports. If you add a location
      to an entry, or use the Nearby tab, the coordinates are sent to Blipfoto as part of that
      request. The developer does not receive a copy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Photos are uploaded as they are, including any details your phone saved in them, such as
      the camera model and where the photo was taken. That goes to Blipfoto with the photo.
    </p>

    <h2>Your location</h2>
    <p>The app uses your location for two things only:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Maps</strong> — to centre a map, or the Nearby tab, on where you are, when you
        ask it to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Photos</strong> — to attach a location to a photo you post, so you can choose to
        show it on Blipfoto's map. The location comes from the photo itself, or from your phone if
        you choose to add where you are now. It stays on your phone, in your draft, until you
        post, and is not shown on the map unless you turn on Add to map.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you say no when your phone asks for location, everything still works, just without
      those features.
    </p>

    <h2>Permissions the app asks for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Camera</strong> — only when you choose to take a photo for an entry.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Location</strong> — only for the two uses described under Your location above.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notifications</strong> — optional; to alert you to new comments and activity,
        and to remind you to post.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Internet</strong> — to reach Blipfoto and the services below.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The app does not ask for access to your whole photo library; you pick photos with Android's
      own picker, which shares only the ones you choose.
    </p>

    <h2>Maps</h2>
    <p>
      Map screens load map tiles from MapTiler. When you open a map, MapTiler receives your
      device's IP address and the map area you are viewing, under
      <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/privacy-policy/">MapTiler's privacy policy</a>. If you
      tap my-location, the map moves to your position, so the area MapTiler receives is the one
      around you. Nothing is sent to MapTiler until you open a map.
    </p>

    <h2>Notifications (optional)</h2>
    <p>
      If you turn notifications on, the app registers with b-push, a small service run by the
      developer on Cloudflare Workers. To check for new activity it stores:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>your Blipfoto user ID;</li>
      <li>
        a read-only Blipfoto access token, encrypted at rest (it can read, but not post, change
        or delete anything);
      </li>
      <li>
        your device's push token and platform, which Google's Firebase Cloud Messaging issues so
        a message can reach your phone;
      </li>
      <li>
        which kinds of notification you chose, how often to check, and the last counts of unread
        comments and notifications.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      b-push only reads <em>counts</em> of unread items from Blipfoto, never their content, and
      never writes to Blipfoto. The push messages it sends are delivered via Google's Firebase
      Cloud Messaging and contain no comment or notification text, only which kind of activity
      occurred, how many new items there are, and your Blipfoto user ID. Push delivery relies on
      Google's Firebase Cloud Messaging. The Firebase code in the app gives your device a
      Firebase installation ID and push token, and Google may log technical data about the
      device, its IP address and message delivery, under Google's own policies and outside the
      developer's control. Cloudflare, as host of b-push, likewise processes technical data such
      as IP addresses under its own policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Deleting it:</strong> turning notifications off for an account, or disconnecting
      the account in the app, deletes its b-push record immediately. If you uninstall the app or
      clear its data without first turning notifications off or disconnecting your account, the
      record is deleted automatically the next time b-push tries to reach your device and Google
      reports it gone.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your access to Blipfoto is revoked, the notification record is deleted as soon as the
      service notices.
    </p>

    <h3>Logs and monitoring</h3>
    <p>
      b-push writes operational logs, kept in Cloudflare's Workers Logs, which the developer
      reads for fault-finding and performance monitoring. They record:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        a summary of each check (how many registrations were checked, how many notifications
        were sent, how many errors);
      </li>
      <li>
        when a check for a registration fails, or a registration is removed: a random
        registration ID that b-push generated, plus an error code and message;
      </li>
      <li>the method and path of requests that failed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Workers Logs keeps them for up to 7 days. They never contain access tokens, request
      bodies, your Blipfoto username or user ID, or the text of any notification. A registration
      ID is a pseudonym: the developer can match it to your b-push record while that record
      exists, so in that sense a log line can be tied to a user, but it identifies nothing else
      about you, and it stops meaning anything once the record is deleted. Cloudflare may also
      keep its own standard request data (such as time, URL and IP address) for the Worker,
      under Cloudflare's policy and for a period set by its plan. The developer does not share
      b-push logs with anyone else, and cannot see what Google keeps.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app does not do</h2>
    <p>
      No advertising, no analytics, no tracking, and no crash-reporting service of its own.
      Google Play may report crash and performance data (Android vitals) to the developer for
      devices where you have allowed that, under Google's policies. The developer does not sell
      or share your data. Apart from the optional notification service above, the app has no
      server of its own.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      The app is not directed at children. Use of Blipfoto is subject to Blipfoto's own terms
      and age requirements.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Everything the app holds is on your device and under your control, apart from the optional
      b-push record described above, which you can delete yourself at any time by turning
      notifications off or disconnecting the account. Data held by Blipfoto is managed through
      Blipfoto. Step-by-step instructions, including where to delete a Blipfoto account, are on
      the <a href="b-mobile-data-deletion.html">data deletion page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes materially, the updated version will be published at this URL with
      a revised date at the top.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
